{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinFromHecke","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinFromHecke","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Notation","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Rat","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NoncommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":26,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.exists_isHeckeGenerator_ne_algebraMap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.exists_isHeckeGenerator_ne_algebraMap","kind":"theorem","line":441,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L441-L452","doc":"A fully concrete, non-degenerate instance: over `ℚ` with `q = 2` and\n`qi = 1/2`, the matrix `!![2, 1; 0, -1/2]` is a Hecke generator, is not a scalar,\nand the two eigenvalues `q = 2` and `-qi = -1/2` are distinct — so the\nfactorisation `(T - 2)(T + 1/2) = 0` really is a factorisation into two different\nlinear factors, not a repeated root. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.witness2_ne_algebraMap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.witness2_ne_algebraMap","kind":"theorem","line":430,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L430-L437","doc":"The `2 × 2` witness is **not a scalar**: it is not in the image of\n`algebraMap`, because its `(0,1)` entry is `1` while every scalar matrix has\n`(0,1)` entry `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_witness2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_witness2","kind":"theorem","line":417,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L417-L428","doc":"The `2 × 2` witness satisfies the Hecke quadratic relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.witness2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.witness2","kind":"def","line":408,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L408-L415","doc":"A **non-scalar** witness: the upper-triangular matrix with the two\neigenvalues on the diagonal.\n\n  `!![q, 1; 0, -qi]`\n\nIts existence is what shows the theorems above are not secretly about scalars —\nsee `witness2_ne_algebraMap`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_right","kind":"theorem","line":399,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L399-L406","doc":"The **second eigenvalue** `-qi` is likewise a Hecke generator: the sign\nrepresentation. That `IsHeckeGenerator` has two distinct scalar solutions is the\ncontent of the eigenvalue factorisation, seen from the other side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_left","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_left","kind":"theorem","line":391,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L391-L397","doc":"The **first eigenvalue** `q`, in `R` viewed as an algebra over itself, is a\nHecke generator. This is the trivial one-dimensional representation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.mirror_mirror","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.mirror_mirror","kind":"theorem","line":377,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L377-L380","doc":"The mirror is an involution. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.mirror_eq_neg_negCrossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.mirror_eq_neg_negCrossing","kind":"theorem","line":372,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L372-L375","doc":"The mirror is the negative of the negative crossing: `h - T = -(T - h)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_mirror","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_mirror","kind":"theorem","line":345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L345-L370","doc":"**`-T⁻¹` is again a Hecke generator, with the same parameters.**\n\nWriting `h = q - qi`, the element `h - T` equals `-(T - h) = -T⁻¹`\n(`mirror_eq_neg_negCrossing`), and it satisfies the *same* quadratic relation\nthat `T` does. The eigenvalue reading explains why: `T` has eigenvalues\n`{q, -qi}`, so `T⁻¹` has `{qi, -q}` and `-T⁻¹` has `{-qi, q}` — the same pair\nback again.\n\nTwo honesty notes on that paragraph. The eigenvalue reading is a *heuristic* and\nit silently assumes `q * qi = 1` (it inverts `q` and `-qi`); the **theorem** does\nnot, and carries no such hypothesis — the proof is the direct expansion\n`(h - T)² = h² - 2hT + T² = h² - hT + 1 = h(h - T) + 1`, valid for any `qi`. And\n\"mirror image of a crossing\" is an interpretation, not a formalised\ncorrespondence; the statement is about ring elements. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.factored_swap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.factored_swap","kind":"theorem","line":326,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L326-L341","doc":"The two linear factors annihilate `T` in either order. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_iff_factored","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_iff_factored","kind":"theorem","line":306,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L306-L324","doc":"**The eigenvalue factorisation, as an equivalence.**\n\nFor mutually inverse scalars `q`, `qi`, the Hecke quadratic relation\n`T² = (q - qi)T + 1` holds **iff** `(T - q)(T + qi) = 0`.\n\nThe right-hand form is what licenses the phrase \"`T` has eigenvalues `q` and\n`-qi`\", and multiplying it through by `T⁻¹` is the classical derivation of the\nskein relation. Stating it as an `iff` — rather than as a one-way consequence of\nthe quadratic — records that the quadratic and the factored form carry the same\ninformation.\n\nThis is an equivalence between the quadratic and the **factored** form; it is\n`isHeckeGenerator_iff_sub_inv` that states the equivalence between the quadratic\nand the **skein relation** itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.factored_expand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.factored_expand","kind":"theorem","line":287,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L287-L304","doc":"The expansion underlying the factorisation. Both the forward and the reverse\ndirection of `isHeckeGenerator_iff_factored` are read off this one identity, and\nso is `factored_swap`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit_sub_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit_sub_inv","kind":"theorem","line":277,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L277-L281","doc":"The skein relation for the packaged unit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_iff_sub_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_iff_sub_inv","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L265-L275","doc":"**The skein relation *is* the Hecke relation**, for an element that has a\nright inverse.\n\nThis is the equivalence the file's title claims, stated where it is actually\ntrue: fix any `S` with `T * S = 1`; then `T` satisfies the Hecke quadratic iff\n`T - S = (q - qi)`. Forward is `sub_inv_eq`, backward is\n`isHeckeGenerator_of_sub_inv`. The hypothesis `T * S = 1` is not removable — for\nan element with no inverse there is no `L₋` to subtract. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_of_sub_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isHeckeGenerator_of_sub_inv","kind":"theorem","line":247,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L247-L263","doc":"**The converse of the skein relation.**\n\nIf `S` is a right inverse of `T` and the difference `T - S` is the scalar\n`q - qi`, then `T` satisfies the Hecke quadratic. Multiplying `T - S = q - qi`\nthrough by `T` on the right and using `T * S = 1` returns the quadratic; the\nright-inverse hypothesis is doing real work and cannot be dropped, since without\nit `T - S = q - qi` says nothing about `T²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.sub_inv_eq_smul_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.sub_inv_eq_smul_one","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L240-L245","doc":"The skein relation with the smoothing `L₀ = 1` made visible:\n\n`T - T⁻¹ = (q - qi) • 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.sub_inv_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.sub_inv_eq","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L224-L238","doc":"**The skein relation.** `L₊ - L₋ = (q - q⁻¹) · L₀`.\n\nStated against an *arbitrary* right inverse `S` of `T`, not against the\nparticular element `negCrossing q qi T`. That is the content: a right inverse of\na Hecke generator is forced to equal `T - (q - qi)` — it cannot be anything else\n— so the difference `T - S` is determined by the parameters alone and the\nrelation is independent of how the negative crossing is presented. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit_inv","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L219-L220","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit_val","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit_val","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L216-L217","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isUnit_of_isHeckeGenerator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.isUnit_of_isHeckeGenerator","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L209-L214","doc":"**A Hecke generator is a unit**, with no hypothesis on `q` whatsoever.\n\n`A` is not assumed commutative, so this needs both of `mul_negCrossing` and\n`negCrossing_mul`; a one-sided inverse would not suffice. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.heckeUnit","kind":"def","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L201-L207","doc":"The unit of `A` determined by a Hecke generator, with its inverse given\nexplicitly rather than existentially. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.negCrossing_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.negCrossing_mul","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L195-L199","doc":"`(T - (q - qi)) · T = 1`: the negative crossing is a left inverse. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.mul_negCrossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.mul_negCrossing","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L187-L193","doc":"`T · (T - (q - qi)) = 1`: the negative crossing is a right inverse. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.negCrossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.negCrossing","kind":"def","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L176-L183","doc":"The **negative crossing** `T - (q - qi)`.\n\nNamed for its role in the skein dictionary: it is the inverse of `T` whenever\n`T` is a Hecke generator (`mul_negCrossing`, `negCrossing_mul`), and the\ndictionary reads `T = L₊`, `negCrossing q qi T = L₋`. That reading is an\ninterpretation; the inverse property is the theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.IsHeckeGenerator.eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.IsHeckeGenerator.eq","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L163-L167","doc":"Accessor: unfold `IsHeckeGenerator` to the underlying equation.\n\nThis is definitional unfolding, not a mathematical claim. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.IsHeckeGenerator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SkeinRelation.IsHeckeGenerator","kind":"def","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SkeinFromHecke.lean#L149-L161","doc":"`T` satisfies the **Hecke quadratic relation** with parameters `q`, `qi`:\n\n  `T² = (q - qi) · T + 1`.\n\nThis is the symmetric (skein) normalisation of AGENTS.md §7a, in which `qi`\nplays the role of `q⁻¹`; the Iwahori normalisation `(T - q)(T + 1) = 0` is a\ndifferent, rescaled convention and is *not* what this predicate says.\n\nThe parameter `qi` is deliberately unconstrained here — `q * qi = 1` is required\nonly where the eigenvalue factorisation is used, and stating the predicate\nwithout it keeps the invertibility results below free of that hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0}]}